SET ANSI_NULLS ON;

UPDATE Beats
SET Text = N'He didn''t open it.

Yemina watched him carry the folder to a spot against the wall of the second bay, out of the main path of the hand-trucks, and set the hand-truck he''d been using down against the same wall, and stand there. He held the folder in both hands at chest height, the way you hold a thing you''ve been handed at a funeral, and he looked at the barcode on its face, and he did not break the seal.

Her hand went to her wrist out of a habit she hadn''t consciously started, and she was timing him before she noticed she''d begun. The same reflex that had run underneath the entire case — the flag against the departure, the query latency, the interval between a call placed and a call answered — the part of her that could not stand in the presence of a duration without measuring it. A forklift passed behind him close enough that its mirror nearly caught his shoulder and he didn''t flinch. His grip hadn''t tightened and hadn''t loosened. He was holding it the way you hold something whose weight you have already accepted.

She had expected — she realized this only now, watching him not do it — that a man handed an unopened case file at a dock gate would want to know what was in it. Curiosity was the ordinary human response to a sealed thing arriving without explanation. He showed none of it. He wasn''t fighting the urge to look. There was no visible urge. Whatever was being asked of him, he seemed to have understood it before the folder reached his hands, and the not-opening wasn''t restraint. It was already the whole answer.

A coworker called something to him — a question about a load, ordinary, work continuing around him with no awareness that anything had stopped — and he answered without lowering the folder, in a voice that carried none of the strain his stillness suggested. She had a word for what she would have felt in that position. Watching him, she couldn''t make the word fit. Whatever arrangement had brought the folder to his hands, he had already accounted for it before it arrived. The stillness wasn''t decision. It was what came after — the part where you hold what you agreed to hold, until it''s time to put it down.

Four minutes, near enough. She let it go.

For the first time in three weeks of gaps — four minutes here, eleven there, every interval in this case a fact she''d logged and filed and cross-referenced — she did not write the number down. She had no form it belonged on.

He straightened. He looked, for just a moment, toward the gate — not at her, she was certain of that, there was no recognition in it, nothing that suggested he knew a woman by a gray car had been counting his stillness in seconds — and then he turned and carried the folder toward the lockers at the back of the bay.',
    UpdatedAt = GETUTCDATE()
WHERE Id = '019F2A49-A43B-7179-8A32-514F835C5569';
